\f1\fs22 Johnny had hardly lifted his head from his work since Sarah had laid down the law 2 months prior. He had resolved to hunker down and produce results before turning his mind to revenge. He would do as he was told and keep complaints to a minimum. He calculated a total absence of complaints would be noticed, so he would throw in a few here an there just to keep in character. He even managed to talk Dr Kathy into hiring a cryptologist, a mathematician, an Egyptologist, several varieties of physicists and and a few other boffins to his team of techs and accelerate the analysis. Although he was too self-absorbed to notice, the improved work ethic lead to actual efficiency, productivity and a degree of work place harmony. His underlings didn\'92t even want to kill him anymore. A decent maiming would more than suffice. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eight weeks of hard slog by the entire team had confirmed the markings in the Beta-mode identifier pattern were indeed some sort of message. The oscillations in the blurred marking were not random but patterns repeated in parallel lines, many times over. Initially, it was hoped the message would be found in the manner of repetition, not unlike some heavenly Morse code, but those hopes were soon dashed. Simplistic thinking would not crack this code.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Could it be a musical scale?\'94 Asked Senior Technical Officer Vilnius Cambridge at the daily briefing. A hail of notebooks and palm devices bounced off his forehead. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ve tried that.\'94 Johnny said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What about a logarithmic scale?\'94 Vilnius replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Give the music scales a break will you?\'94 For the moment Johnny kept his cool.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, we\'92ve got all the computer processing power in the world, making a billion calculations a second and for the last 2 months we have made virtually no progress.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think it has something to do with gas chromatography.\'94 Said the physicist, Rosalind Frankland. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, light spectroscopy.\'94 Argued the resident astrophysicist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have either of you constructed a viable method of testing?\'94 Johnny asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No.\'94 They both replied. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Egyptian Hieroglyphs were thought meaningless until the discovery of the Rosetta Stone.\'94 Egyptologist\cf3  \cf2 added.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thanks for the history lesson, but how does that help?\'94 Johnny asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Perhaps we need a cypher key or code phrase.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, I\'92ll order one today.\'94 Johnny snapped back. \'93Problem solved.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93For the translation we wish to make, there is no telling what the equivalent of a Rosetta Stone is, but if it can be identified, the message will translate itself.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So what you are saying is, we are searching for that elusive clue that will unlock the mystery?\'94 Johnny managed to remain calm.\uc0\u8232 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So how does that differ from what we are already doing?\'94 Johnny screamed at the history buff. \'93We are going to solve this with hard work, trial and error, plenty of error and some luck. Let\'92s get to it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The next morning the senior management team met as usual for the morning briefing. Vilnius Cambridge\cf4  \cf2 was nursing a few cuts and bruises, courtesy of yesterday\'92s briefing and had resolved to remain silent. Particle Physicist, Dr Bradley Quark, was fidgeting and could not wait for Johnny to open the meeting. \'93I have been working on imaging. At ultra microscopic level the message have thousands of parallel markings with blurred, but delineated micro-grooves, sort of like a recording on those old black dics.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Like they used to record music on\'94 Vilnius felt vindicated until the first notepad struck the bridge of his nose. He managed to duck the rest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You see people, our physicist has made an interesting observation. It may amount to nothing, but it is worth following up.\'94 Johnny winced. He was not used to praising anyone, apart from himself. \'93Much better than Rosetta stone platitudes.\'94\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ve got more news.\'94 The physicist said. \'93The images are blurred because they are slightly out of phase. If as you claim this is message is from before the big bang, I thought it may be from another universe, a universe where the universal gravitational constant is different.\'94\cf4  \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf4 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There are 26 fundamental constants that describe the universe, how did you decide upon the gravitational constant?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The discs are said to operate on gravity waves, I just figured it was a good starting point. I just got lucky.\'94 The physicist admitted. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How lucky?\'94 Johnny was actually interested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have only had time to do a quick analysis of two messages, but there are some interesting findings.\'94 He switch on the holo-screen and pointed to an equation floating in the space before them. \'93As you know the universal gravitational constant is expressed by\'85\'85.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>{\*\shppict{\pict {\*\nisusfilename Clipboard Image}\picw586\pich80\picwgoal8790\pichgoal1200\pngblip }}\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'85\'85I filtered the images through a phase variance compensator and I have calculated, from the degree of variance, this message is from a universe with a slightly higher universal gravitational constant. When I tried to replicate the process with another message, I found the phase variance was different again, with the Universal Gravitational constant slightly lower than our universe.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good work, doctor.\'94 Johnny tried to be cool but he was reeling. 
\f2\i Different universes!
\f1\i0  \'93Congratulations, Dr Quark. We can actually report some real progress. How long until you can examine all of the messages?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, if we share the load equally, we should only have 500 or 600 each to examine. If we can get more two more phase variance compensators, we should be finished before the end of the month.\'94 Dr Quark replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Excellent.\'94 Johnny stood and leaned across the table, offering his hand to Dr Quark. The Doctor took it hesitantly, but firmly and glanced at his colleagues, each of whom wore jaws agape. \'93You have made my day.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you, Johnny\'94 Dr Quark replied. Johnny halted the handshake, mid-pump. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What of the other 25 universal constants? The fine structure constant? The strong coupling constant? Neutrino mixing Parameters? Quark Mixing parameters?\'94 Johnny stopped at that one. He saw a few of the lab coats chuckle. 
\f2\i You don\'92t get to call me Johnny. 
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am well aware of the quark mixing parameters.\'94 Dr Quark sighed. He had had his fill of quark jokes. \'93I have not been able to cast my mind to the other constants, but my impression is they are probably not too different. Also, I would think if gravity is different in the other universes, time may also pass at a different rate.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93When you have checked all of the messages, start on the other universal constants and get back to me.\'94 Johnny left the room without another word.\

